From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 10 Nov 2026 16:50:16 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] um: walk the mconsole's console clients under their lock

A management-console command that runs through with_console(), "sysrq"
or "stack", adds an entry from its own stack to the console's client
list and removes it when the command is done, both under client_lock.
The console's write() walked the list without the lock. On an SMP
guest another CPU that owns the console keeps printing while the
command runs on the CPU that took the console's interrupt: when that
CPU removes its entry and returns, the walk still holds the entry,
whose stack slot the next frame zeroes, reads a NULL next pointer and
faults at address 0x10 in console_write(). A host that asks for
"sysrq s" while the guest prints panics it.

Hold client_lock across the walk, which also serializes the shared
console_buf. Interrupts are already off there, and nothing in it
waits for the CPU that holds the lock. While an oops or a panic is in
progress the CPU holding it may have stopped, so the console only tries
the lock and writes nothing without it. with_console() now removes its
entry before the final reply, so no console text follows that reply.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 arch/um/drivers/mconsole_kern.c | 21 +++++++++++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 19 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/arch/um/drivers/mconsole_kern.c b/arch/um/drivers/mconsole_kern.c
index 6ff80357a47ff93f6459f0a4515610e695d59f7a..3f7d0caf9915a4fc6ebf4a87e992a1d59467a9f7 100644
--- a/arch/um/drivers/mconsole_kern.c
+++ b/arch/um/drivers/mconsole_kern.c
@@ -694,11 +694,25 @@ static void console_write(struct console *console, const char *string,
 			  unsigned int len)
 {
 	struct list_head *ele;
+	unsigned long flags;
 	int n;
 
 	if (list_empty(&clients))
 		return;
 
+	/*
+	 * An entry lives on the stack of a with_console() that may run on
+	 * another CPU, so only client_lock keeps it there while the walk
+	 * replies to it. A CPU that an oops or a panic stopped may hold the
+	 * lock; the console then writes nothing rather than wait for it.
+	 */
+	if (oops_in_progress) {
+		if (!spin_trylock_irqsave(&client_lock, flags))
+			return;
+	} else {
+		spin_lock_irqsave(&client_lock, flags);
+	}
+
 	while (len > 0) {
 		n = min((size_t) len, ARRAY_SIZE(console_buf));
 		memcpy(console_buf, string, n);
@@ -712,6 +726,8 @@ static void console_write(struct console *console, const char *string,
 			mconsole_reply_len(entry->req, console_buf, n, 0, 1);
 		}
 	}
+
+	spin_unlock_irqrestore(&client_lock, flags);
 }
 
 static struct console mc_console = { .name	= "mc",
@@ -740,11 +756,12 @@ static void with_console(struct mc_request *req, void (*proc)(void *),
 
 	(*proc)(arg);
 
-	mconsole_reply_len(req, "", 0, 0, 0);
-
+	/* No console text follows the final reply. */
 	spin_lock_irqsave(&client_lock, flags);
 	list_del(&entry.list);
 	spin_unlock_irqrestore(&client_lock, flags);
+
+	mconsole_reply_len(req, "", 0, 0, 0);
 }
 
 #ifdef CONFIG_MAGIC_SYSRQ
